1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,519
Futures gave us results from pools. Those
results often land in shared structures —
caches, metrics maps, work queues —
touched by many threads.

2
00:00:09,639 --> 00:00:23,519
Synchronizing every access to an ordinary
HashMap works until contention makes the
whole map a single-file line. Can the
collection itself offer safe concurrent
access without forcing us to lock the
entire world?

3
00:00:23,699 --> 00:00:36,100
Concurrent collections answer that need.
They make shared structure access safer.
They do not make every multi-step business
operation automatically correct. That
distinction is the whole episode.

4
00:00:36,219 --> 00:00:45,659
Start with the map you will see most: Look
at this java for a moment. merge
atomically associates key "a" with 1 if
absent, or applies Integer::sum if
present.

5
00:00:45,780 --> 00:00:54,660
You did not write a check-then-act
sequence that another thread could
interleave. Single operations on
ConcurrentHashMap are designed for
concurrent use.

6
00:00:54,780 --> 00:01:04,019
The trap is pretending that two separate
calls are one atomic story: Look at this
java for a moment. Between containsKey and
put, another thread can insert.

7
00:01:04,140 --> 00:01:18,260
Use putIfAbsent, compute, merge, and
friends when the logic is compound. Single
ops are safe; multi-step needs atomic APIs
or additional coordination. Copy-on-write
structures trade a different cost.

8
00:01:18,379 --> 00:01:27,579
CopyOnWriteArrayList copies the underlying
array on each mutation, then publishes the
new array. Readers can iterate without
locking and see a snapshot.

9
00:01:27,699 --> 00:01:38,099
That shines when reads dominate and writes
are rare — listener lists are the classic
example. It hurts when writes are
frequent, because every write pays for a
full copy.

10
00:01:38,219 --> 00:01:45,579
Blocking queues belong in the same family
— concurrent collections specialized for
handoffs — and they get their own deep
treatment soon.

11
00:01:45,699 --> 00:01:56,499
Weakly consistent iterators on many
concurrent collections mean an iterator
may reflect some updates and not others —
it will not throw
ConcurrentModificationException the way a
fail-fast ArrayList iterator might,

12
00:01:56,619 --> 00:02:02,219
but it also will not freeze a perfect
point-in-time view unless the collection
promises one.

13
00:02:02,339 --> 00:02:16,379
What if we wrap every ConcurrentHashMap
call in synchronized (map) "to be extra
safe"? Usually you add contention without
adding meaning. The map already
coordinates its single operations.

14
00:02:16,500 --> 00:02:29,500
External synchronization can be necessary
for multi-map invariants — but then you
are designing a higher-level lock
protocol, not improving ConcurrentHashMap.
A small caching story ties the points
together.

15
00:02:29,620 --> 00:02:38,099
Threads look up a computed value by key.
If absent, they compute and insert.
computeIfAbsent keeps that check-and-
create atomic for the key.

16
00:02:38,219 --> 00:02:49,219
If computation is expensive and must not
run twice, that atomic API is correctness
under load. If the value itself is mutable
and shared, you still need a protocol for
mutating it.

17
00:02:49,340 --> 00:03:03,460
The map's concurrency does not bless the
object's interior. CopyOnWrite earns one
concrete picture. A list of listeners is
read on every event and updated when
someone subscribes. Updates are rare;
reads are constant.

18
00:03:03,580 --> 00:03:17,860
Copy-on-write makes reads cheap at the
cost of a copy on subscribe. Invert that
workload — writes constantly, reads rare —
and the copies dominate. The structure did
not fail. The workload did not match the
trade.

19
00:03:17,980 --> 00:03:25,340
Picture a feature-flag map read on every
request and updated by a rare admin call.
ConcurrentHashMap fits.

20
00:03:25,460 --> 00:03:36,819
An admin update that changes three related
keys under one logical "release" may still
need a higher-level protocol — a single
versioned immutable snapshot published
atomically, for example.

21
00:03:36,939 --> 00:03:49,219
The collection keeps single-key operations
honest; your product invariants may still
need a wider story. A metrics counter map
is a good weekly exercise. Increment with
merge. Read with ordinary gets.

22
00:03:49,340 --> 00:03:57,340
Resist synchronizing around the whole map
"just in case" unless you are composing a
multi-key invariant the map cannot
express.

23
00:03:57,460 --> 00:04:08,340
Sometimes the shared state is not a map of
many keys — it is a single counter or flag
updated constantly. Locking may be too
heavy; check-then-act on a plain int is
too racy.

24
00:04:08,460 --> 00:04:11,580
That hotspot shape is where atomics earn
their keep.
